Why vendored: the workload build is JDK-only (no Maven or Gradle) and must work
offline. The core workload stays free of third-party code (docs/decisions.md
#5); only this separate module's jar, workload-libs.jar, runs against
these jars, named in its manifest's Class-Path, not copied into it. `mage
workloadLibsBuild` checks the SHA-256s above before compiling.

Why Tomcat: one jar holds both the container and the Servlet API, so the
scenario needs no further dependency (Jetty would need half a dozen).

Why this version: 10.1.61 is the newest 10.1 release at least two weeks
old on the fetch date (10.1.62 was 9 days old). 10.1 is the jakarta
namespace on a Java 11 floor, so it runs on every JDK the agent supports
(decision #29: 17+); Tomcat 11 would need 17 too but changes nothing the
hook reads.

To update: download both jars of the new version from Maven Central,
compare each SHA-1 with the published .sha1, replace the jars and the
license files, and update the version, the hashes here and libsJars in
magefile_libs.go in the same commit.
